WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.699
You want to know where feeSlow spends its CPU time, without changing its code.

2
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.382
Here is the thread's call stack.

3
00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:12.737
Thread.run is the root, at the bottom.

4
00:00:12.737 --> 00:00:15.834
Each method sits on top of the one that called it.

5
00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:18.560
The top frame is where the CPU is right now.

6
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.914
The stack changes all the time.

7
00:00:20.914 --> 00:00:26.415
A sampling profiler is a loop: every 10 ms, it takes a snapshot of the stack.

8
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.411
Then it counts identical stacks.

9
00:00:30.411 --> 00:00:35.887
One line per stack, with its count: this text is what every flame graph tool reads.

10
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:42.315
To draw a flame graph, the tool first sorts the stacks alphabetically, by full class name.

11
00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:46.183
Not by time: the sample from 20 ms ends up on the right.

12
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.871
Then identical frames at the same depth, under the same parent, merge into one box.

13
00:00:51.871 --> 00:00:54.179
When each sample was taken is gone.

14
00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.776
So each box is one frame, and its width is the number of samples with that frame on the stack.

15
00:01:00.776 --> 00:01:02.947
String.format was on 4 of the 9.

16
00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:06.651
feeSlow was on all 9, so it spans the whole width.

17
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:10.439
And left to right is the alphabet, not a timeline.

18
00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:16.011
flamegraph.pl's own header says it: the ordering on the x-axis has no meaning.

19
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:17.669
Now look at the tops.

20
00:01:17.669 --> 00:01:23.892
The part of a box with nothing above it is self time: the CPU was in that frame's own code.

21
00:01:23.892 --> 00:01:25.875
Wide tops are where to start.

22
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:28.995
feeSlow is wide, but covered by its children.

23
00:01:28.995 --> 00:01:34.656
It does little itself and calls expensive things, so the fix is usually in one of them.

24
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:43.331
This is feeSlow in a real CPU profile of the same fee code, zoomed in.

25
00:01:43.331 --> 00:01:47.899
It covered 5,747 of 8,005 samples: 71.8%.

26
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.918
Walk up from that wide plateau.

27
00:01:50.918 --> 00:01:59.825
Its widest children are String.format, with 23.3% of all samples, and Pattern.compile, with 17.6%.

28
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:07.635
Then toPlainString, with 11.5%, and the BigDecimal constructor, with 7.9%.

29
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:11.252
Pattern.compile builds a regex on every call.

30
00:02:11.252 --> 00:02:18.248
Move it into a static final field, and that whole tower, about 18% of the samples, goes away.

31
00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:19.667
Width is samples.

32
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:21.844
Left to right is the alphabet.

33
00:02:21.844 --> 00:02:24.384
And the tops are where the CPU was.
